  "primary_subject_agency_ids": [],
  "summary": "Major retailers including Home Depot, Lowe's, Costco, Target and Walmart say they use ALPRs responsibly with privacy safeguards, but privacy advocates and community groups argue the deployments lack public notice, oversight, and meaningful restrictions on data-sharing \u2014 including potential access by ICE through local police partnerships. Shareholders at Home Depot and Lowe's recently voted down proposals seeking privacy risk reports on ALPR use.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Records of Flock shared networks obtained by this news outlet indicate that Lowe's and Home Depot stores in the state provide at least some local police agencies with access to their stores' ALPR databases.",
      "paragraph_idx": 7
    },
    {
      "quote": "We do not grant access to our license plate readers to federal law enforcement and we have not authorized any law enforcement agency other than local and state law enforcement agencies to access our ALPR data",
      "paragraph_idx": 13
    },
    {
      "quote": "police agencies can search through these databases without a warrant for untold numbers of reasons, including immigration",
      "paragraph_idx": 21
    },
    {
      "quote": "local and state laws are \"desperately needed\" to regulate ALPRs, such as restrictions on data-sharing by non-government organizations",
      "paragraph_idx": 27
    }
  ],
